---
import Base from '../../layouts/Base.astro';
import Disclosure from '../../components/Disclosure.astro';
import EmailCapture from '../../components/EmailCapture.astro';
import { url } from '../../utils/url';
import pumpData from '../../data/pumps.json';
import { recommend, type Pump } from '../../utils/pump-calc';
import { estimateElectricity } from '../../utils/cost-calc';

const headline = "AC pump vs DC pump: reef return pumps compared";
const reference = { displayGallons: 75, turnover: 7, staticHeadFt: 4.5, pipeIdInches: 1.029, pipeLengthFt: 10, elbows: 4 };
const { targetGPH, results } = recommend(reference, pumpData.pumps as Pump[]);
const comparison = ['AC', 'DC'].map((type) => results.find((r) => r.pump.type === type && r.meetsTarget && r.pump.watts != null && r.pump.approxPriceUSD != null)!);
const annualCost = (watts: number) => estimateElectricity({ watts, hoursPerDay: 24, days: 365, kwhRate: 0.17 }).cost;
---
<Base
  headline={headline}
  title="AC Pump vs DC Pump for Reef Tanks"
  description="Compare DC and AC reef return pumps for flow control, noise, power use, price, and failure risks. Find out which type fits your plumbing and budget."
  breadcrumbs={[{ name: 'Guides', href: '/guides' }, { name: 'DC vs AC Return Pumps', href: '/guides/dc-vs-ac-return-pumps' }]}
>
  <section class="container prose" style="padding-top:40px;">
    <p class="eyebrow" style="color:var(--brand-2)">Decision guide</p>
    <h1>{headline}</h1>
    <p class="lede-dark">Choose a DC return pump for adjustable speed and feed modes; choose an AC return pump when fixed-speed operation and fewer external control parts suit the build. Neither type wins on its label alone: compare delivered flow at your head height, power draw, replacement parts, and total cost.</p>

    <Disclosure />

    <h2 id="quick-answer">AC vs DC: the short answer</h2>
    <p><strong>DC:</strong> speed control helps match the overflow and provides feed modes, but adds a controller and power supply to the replacement-parts list. <strong>AC:</strong> fixed-speed models are simpler to operate, but need careful sizing. Buy for the flow you need after plumbing losses; keep a compatible spare or a recovery plan whichever type you choose.</p>

    <h2>A 75-gallon example from our pump data</h2>
    <p>These are the smallest qualifying AC and DC pumps with recorded watts and prices in the calculator's ordered results. Both must reach {targetGPH} GPH after {reference.staticHeadFt} ft of lift, {reference.pipeLengthFt} ft of pipe with a {reference.pipeIdInches}-inch inside diameter, and {reference.elbows} elbows. Data date: {pumpData.generated}; prices have not been refreshed.</p>
    <div class="tablewrap"><table class="breakdown">
      <thead><tr><th>Type / model</th><th>Modeled delivered flow</th><th>Rated watts</th><th>Recorded price</th><th>Power / year</th></tr></thead>
      <tbody>{comparison.map((r) => <tr><td>{r.pump.type}: {r.pump.brand} {r.pump.model}</td><td>{r.deliveredGPH} GPH</td><td>{r.pump.watts}W</td><td>${r.pump.approxPriceUSD?.toFixed(2)}</td><td>${annualCost(r.pump.watts!).toFixed(2)}</td></tr>)}</tbody>
    </table></div>
    <p class="rnote">Power cost assumes rated watts for 24 hours × 365 days at $0.17/kWh. It is an estimate, not a measured bill or an equal-flow efficiency test. Flow may use a modeled curve where marked in the <a href={url('/reviews/best-return-pumps')}>full return pump comparison</a>. Enter measured watts and your own rate in the <a href={url('/tools/reef-build-cost-calculator') + '#aquarium-electricity-cost-calculator'}>aquarium electricity cost calculator</a>.</p>

    <h2>Controllability</h2>
    <p>A controllable DC pump lets you change speed without changing plumbing. Check whether the model supports feed mode and your aquarium controller before buying. A fixed-speed AC pump may allow outlet throttling, depending on its manual. Do not restrict the inlet or assume every pump responds the same way.</p>

    <h2>Noise</h2>
    <p>DC pumps are usually noticeably quieter, especially at the reduced speeds most people actually run. AC pumps hum at full tilt all the time. In a bedroom or living-room tank, that difference matters a lot more than the spec sheet suggests.</p>

    <h2>Efficiency</h2>
    <p>Compare watts at the flow your system needs. A DC model turned down may use less power than an oversized fixed-speed model, but a DC badge alone does not establish savings. Every 10W of continuous draw uses 87.6 kWh in 365 days — ${annualCost(10).toFixed(2)} at the example rate. Measure the installed draw before claiming a payback period.</p>

    <h2>Price and reliability</h2>
    <p>Check the actual model price rather than assuming AC is always cheaper. A DC controller and power supply add replaceable parts; an AC pump can still fail through wear, blockage, or restart trouble. This dataset has specifications, not a controlled failure-rate study. Test a power-off restart with your plumbing and keep strainers and impellers clean.</p>

    <h2>The failure-mode angle most people miss</h2>
    <p>If a DC controller dies, the pump stops — full stop — until you replace the controller. An AC pump has no controller to die. That's why a lot of experienced reefers run DC as their daily driver but keep a cheap AC pump boxed up as a same-day backup. Redundancy is cheaper than a tank crash.</p>

    <h2>So: which, for your build?</h2>
    <p>Whichever you choose, the number that matters isn't the box GPH — it's the real flow at <em>your</em> head height and plumbing. Size it properly:</p>
    <p><a href={url('/tools/return-pump-calculator')} class="inlinecta">→ Size your return pump (DC and AC, ranked together)</a></p>
    <p style="margin-top:-6px;"><a href={url('/reviews/best-return-pumps')}>See our full return pump rankings →</a></p>
  </section>

  <section class="container">
    <EmailCapture />
  </section>
</Base>

<style>
  .lede-dark { margin-bottom: 8px; }
  .inlinecta { display:inline-block; font-family:var(--font-display); font-weight:700; color:var(--brand-2); }
  .tablewrap { overflow-x:auto; }
  .tablewrap table { min-width:640px; }
</style>
